__d(function(e,t,o,i,a,n,h){a.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:6,subject:"English",textbook:"Honeysuckle",chapter_number:11,chapter_title:"Vocation",poet:"Rabindranath Tagore",total_cards:20,cards:[{id:"fc01",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Who wrote the poem 'Vocation'?",back:"The poem 'Vocation' was written by Rabindranath Tagore \u2014 the great Indian poet, philosopher, and author who was the first Asian to win the Nobel Prize in Literature, in 1913. He wrote in Bengali and his works have been translated into many languages including Kannada.",memory_hook:"TAGORE = Tremendous Artist Gifting Outstanding Rhymes Everywhere. His Nobel Prize in 1913 makes him India's most celebrated poet!"},{id:"fc02",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Who is the speaker of the poem 'Vocation'? What three workers does the speaker observe?",back:"The speaker of the poem is a school-going child (a young boy). He observes three workers during his day: (1) a hawker selling bangles in the morning, (2) a gardener digging in the afternoon, and (3) a watchman walking the street in the evening.",memory_hook:"HGW \u2014 Hawker, Gardener, Watchman. Or remember it as: 'Hard-working, Grimy, Wakeful' \u2014 all three are workers the child envies!"},{id:"fc03",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does the hawker in the poem sell? What does he cry out?",back:"The hawker sells bangles \u2014 specifically crystal bangles. He walks the roads calling out 'Bangles, crystal bangles!' to attract buyers. The hawker has no fixed path, no fixed time, and no place he must go to \u2014 he is free to roam as he pleases.",memory_hook:"'Bangles, crystal bangles!' \u2014 even reading it feels like you can hear a street vendor! The hawker's call is the most musical line in the poem."},{id:"fc04",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the simile used to describe the street-lamp in the poem?",back:"The street-lamp is compared to 'a giant with one red eye in its head'. This simile appears in the third stanza when the child watches the dark lane at night. It captures how eerie and large the lamp looks to a child's imagination in the darkness.",memory_hook:"Imagine a Cyclops holding a torch in the middle of a dark street \u2014 that's exactly what Tagore wants you to picture! One red eye = one lamp glowing red in the night."},{id:"fc05",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"At what time does the child leave for school in the poem?",back:"The child leaves for school when 'the gong sounds ten' \u2014 meaning at ten o'clock in the morning. The poem opens with this line, establishing that the child has a fixed time to be somewhere, unlike the hawker who has 'nothing to hurry him on'.",memory_hook:"GONG SOUNDS TEN = School begins then. The gong is like a school bell \u2014 the moment it rings, freedom ends!"},{id:"fc06",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does the word 'vocation' mean? Why is it the poem's title?",back:"'Vocation' means a job, profession, or occupation \u2014 especially one that feels like a calling. It is the poem's title because the child envies the vocations (jobs) of the hawker, gardener, and watchman \u2014 not for the work itself, but for the freedom they seem to bring. The irony is that the child envies adult work precisely because he misunderstands it as freedom.",memory_hook:"VOCation = VOICE + ACTION. A vocation is what you are called (voiced) to do. The child in the poem is being 'called' to envy three vocations he sees around him!"},{id:"fc07",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does 'takes him to task' mean in the poem?",back:"'Takes him to task' is an idiom meaning to scold, reprimand, or hold someone accountable for their actions. In the poem, the gardener soils his clothes and gets wet or baked in the sun \u2014 'nobody takes him to task' means no one scolds him for this. The child, who would certainly be scolded for the same behaviour, envies this freedom from reprimand.",memory_hook:"TASK = Tell And Scold Keenly. Nobody TASKS the gardener \u2014 but the child? Tasked constantly by mother, teacher, everyone!"},{id:"fc08",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is a 'hawker'? Give one detail about the hawker from the poem.",back:"A hawker is a street vendor or peddler who travels from place to place selling goods, usually calling out loudly to attract buyers. In the poem, the hawker sells crystal bangles and cries 'Bangles, crystal bangles!' He has no fixed road, no schedule, and no destination \u2014 he can go wherever he pleases.",memory_hook:"HAWKER = Has Absolute Wandering, Known to Everyone on the Road. A hawker is the original 'work from anywhere' person \u2014 before it was fashionable!"},{id:"fc09",type:"Definition",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is 'imagery' as a poetic device? Give one example from 'Vocation'.",back:"Imagery is descriptive language that creates a vivid mental picture or appeals to the senses (sight, sound, touch, smell). In 'Vocation', Tagore uses imagery throughout: the hawker crying out on the road (sound + sight), the gardener soiling his clothes with dust (touch + sight), and the watchman walking the dark lane with a swinging lantern (sight + movement). All these images make the poem feel real and alive.",memory_hook:"IMAGE-ry = Picture painted with Words. When you read 'the watchman swings his lantern in the dark lane', you can SEE and FEEL it \u2014 that's imagery working!"},{id:"fc10",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What is the central theme of the poem 'Vocation'?",back:"The central theme is a child's longing for freedom from rules, fixed routines, and adult supervision. The child is bound by school timings, homework, and bedtime \u2014 and envies three workers who seem to live without these restrictions. The poem captures a universal childhood emotion: the wish to be free, to roam, to not be told what to do. Tagore voices this with warmth and gentle humour.",memory_hook:"Theme = CHILD WANTS FREEDOM. Three workers, three freedoms, one wishing child \u2014 that is the entire poem in five words!"},{id:"fc11",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How is the poem structured? What is the significance of the three times of day?",back:"The poem has three stanzas, each set at a different time of day: morning (school time), afternoon (homework time), and evening (bedtime). Each stanza follows the same pattern \u2014 the child's restriction, the worker's apparent freedom, and the child's wish ('I wish I were...'). This structure mirrors the child's entire day being governed by rules, showing that the longing for freedom is constant, not just at one moment.",memory_hook:"Morning \u2192 Afternoon \u2192 Evening = School \u2192 Homework \u2192 Bed. The child has NO free time! Three stanzas = three prisons the child imagines escaping!"},{id:"fc12",type:"Concept",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What poetic devices does Tagore use in 'Vocation'? Name at least three with an example each.",back:"Tagore uses: (1) Simile \u2014 'the street-lamp stands like a giant with one red eye in its head'; (2) Repetition \u2014 'I wish I were a hawker / gardener / watchman' (same structure in each stanza); (3) Imagery \u2014 the hawker crying on the road, the dark lane, the swinging lantern; (4) Personification \u2014 the watchman 'walks with his shadow at his side' (shadow treated as a companion).",memory_hook:"SIRP your poem: Simile, Imagery, Repetition, Personification \u2014 all four are present in 'Vocation'. SIRP it slowly and you'll find them all!"},{id:"fc13",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"Why does the child envy the watchman specifically?",back:"The child is sent to bed by his mother as soon as it gets dark in the evening. Through his open window, he sees the watchman walking freely up and down the dark lane with a lantern. The watchman 'never once goes to bed in his life' \u2014 to the child, this seems like the ultimate freedom. While the child must lie in bed in the dark, the watchman is outside, awake, and moving freely in the night.",memory_hook:"Watchman = NO Bedtime, EVER. For a child forced to bed at dark, that is the most magical freedom of all \u2014 like staying up for a festival all year long!"},{id:"fc14",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Easy",front:"How does the poem show the contrast between the child's life and the workers' lives?",back:"The contrast is built through the child's restrictions vs the workers' apparent freedoms. The child: must go to school at a fixed time (hawker has no fixed time), must do lessons (gardener has no supervisor), must sleep at dark (watchman never sleeps). Each stanza places the child's restriction against the worker's freedom side by side, making the contrast vivid and emotionally powerful.",memory_hook:"Child = Locked Clock Life. Workers = Freedom Fellows. Tagore puts them next to each other like two photos \u2014 one in a cage, one in the open sky!"},{id:"fc15",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"The child envies the workers' freedom, but is their life actually free? What does the poem leave unsaid?",back:"The poem leaves unsaid the hardship behind each worker's life. The hawker walks long distances in all weather to earn a living. The gardener does heavy physical labour in sun and mud. The watchman is alone in a dark, lonely lane all night \u2014 it can be frightening and exhausting. The child sees only the surface \u2014 the absence of rules \u2014 but not the real difficulties. This is the poem's gentle irony: what looks like freedom from the outside often comes with its own burdens.",memory_hook:"FREEDOM in the poem = Freedom From Rules, not Freedom From Hardship. The child sees one type of freedom; Tagore is quietly showing us another truth!"},{id:"fc16",type:"Why/How",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Why does Tagore repeat the phrase 'I wish I were' in every stanza?",back:"Tagore uses repetition of 'I wish I were' deliberately. Repetition is a poetic device that reinforces emotion and creates a musical, rhythmic effect. Here, it underscores the child's constant, persistent longing \u2014 the wish does not go away; it is renewed with every encounter with a worker. The repetition also gives the poem a refrain-like quality similar to folk songs and oral poetry, connecting it to India's poetic traditions.",memory_hook:"I WISH I WERE = the child's song. Imagine a child humming the same tune all day: 'I wish, I wish, I wish...' \u2014 that is exactly what Tagore creates with repetition!"},{id:"fc17",type:"Application",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Can you relate to the child in the poem? Describe a situation where you wished you could do something freely, without rules.",back:"This is a personal reflection card. Think of a moment when you wished you were free of a rule \u2014 perhaps wanting to play outside instead of attending tuition, or staying up to watch a match instead of going to bed. The feeling of wishing for someone else's apparent freedom is exactly what Tagore captures. Connect it to a specific rule or routine in your life and describe which 'worker' you would envy (like the child envies the hawker, gardener, watchman).",memory_hook:"Your own 'I wish I were...' moment is the heart of this poem. Everyone has one \u2014 Tagore just wrote it down for all of us!"},{id:"fc18",type:"Application",difficulty:"Moderate",front:"Rewrite the child's wish for the watchman in your own words \u2014 as if you were the child looking through the window.",back:"Model answer: 'It is dark outside. My mother has told me to sleep, but I cannot stop watching the watchman through my window. He walks up and down the lonely lane, his lantern making a small circle of light in the darkness. His shadow follows him like a friend. He will never go to sleep. I wish I were him \u2014 walking free in the night, chasing shadows with my lantern, never having to lie in this bed staring at the ceiling.'",memory_hook:"Rewriting in your own words = true understanding! If you can say it differently but keep the same feeling, you have truly understood Tagore's poem."},{id:"fc19",type:"Application",difficulty:"Hard",front:"The poem is titled 'Vocation'. The child wishes for three vocations. But does the poem suggest that the child's real 'vocation' is something else entirely?",back:"This is a deeper analysis question. The child ends each stanza with a wish \u2014 but the act of observing, feeling, and wishing is itself a creative act. By the end of the poem, the child says 'I'll put it in a poem' (in the Teachers poem \u2014 and similarly here, Tagore implies the child is a poet in the making). The child's real vocation may not be hawking, gardening, or night-watching \u2014 it may be poetry itself. The poem suggests that the act of noticing, imagining, and longing is what makes a poet. Tagore's own childhood experience of longing for freedom is widely believed to be the inspiration for this poem.",memory_hook:"The child's REAL vocation = POET. He does not become the hawker \u2014 he writes about him. Tagore turned his own childhood wishes into Nobel Prize-winning poetry!"},{id:"fc20",type:"Recall",difficulty:"Easy",front:"What does the gardener do that the child envies? List three specific things.",back:"The gardener: (1) does what he likes with his spade \u2014 he has full control over his work, (2) soils his clothes with dust \u2014 he can get dirty without being scolded, and (3) gets baked in the sun or gets wet \u2014 he can work in any weather without anyone stopping him or taking him to task. The child envies all three because he would be reprimanded for any of these behaviours.",memory_hook:"Gardener's Three Freedoms: SPADE freely, SOIL freely, SWEAT freely. The child can do NONE of these without getting into trouble!"}]}},2949,[]);